<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Central Asia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> is the vast inland region between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, including the steppes and the oasis cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>. It was a crossroads of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> or battleground of many empires, from the Achaemenid and Seleucid empires to the Chagatai, Timurid and Khwarazmian empires. In the nineteenth century much of it was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/central-asia.jpg" alt="Central Asia" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Serg!o · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Central_Asia_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
